I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction range/resolution, and the frozen analysis guidance. Then I’ll establish a chemically ordinary multiphase GSAS-II checkpoint before testing any residual families or running the single final polish.

The chemistry is Mn–W–O after a 1 h, 800 °C firing from Mn₃O₄ + WO₃. Likely anchors are manganese tungstate plus residual/decomposition oxides, but I’ll let the observed reflection families and the frozen index decide the inventory. The raw scan has 8,301 points over 9.9–100.1° with Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ and no supplied uncertainties or profile calibration.

The first screening result is decisive for the main phase: the allowed MnWO₄ wolframite reference explains about 78% of the weighted peak signal by itself. Its entire strong family is present with a common ≈+0.35° displacement (for example 18.480→18.857°, 23.708→24.051°, and 30.004/30.448→30.376/30.810°), consistent with the permitted specimen-height/zero range. Several coherent peaks remain—especially near 20.32, 20.88, 29.15, and 35.62°—so this is not yet a one-phase solution.

