The residual is now coherent enough to justify analogue transfer. The strongest framework match is orthorhombic Fe₂WO₆ (space group 60) with Fe→Mn: it independently predicts the unassigned 19.8–20.9° triplet, 35.59°, 54.14°, and 64.77/65.08° families. Mn₂WO₆ is chemically credible here because Mn₃O₄ supplies Mn³⁺; moreover, equal moles of Mn₂WO₆ and residual WO₃ exactly preserve the starting 1:1 Mn:W ratio alongside any MnWO₄. I’m testing that topology as an analogue-derived phase, with its provenance and substitution retained.

The analogue is positive but not yet acceptable: it refines to only ~5 wt% and pushes one cell axis ≈3% from Fe₂WO₆ while its crystallite size hits the 0.04 µm lower bound. That is evidence of partial framework overlap, not proof of Mn₂WO₆. I’m keeping the two-phase checkpoint as the incumbent and using physically bounded profile terms to separate genuine residual reflections from width/intensity errors before accepting or rejecting this third phase.

The bounded two-phase fit exposes a clean, narrow residual family rather than broad intensity error. Headless indexing gives an excellent primitive orthorhombic cell, a = 4.4465, b = 7.7005, c = 10.4610 Å, V = 358.19 Å³ (M20 ≈ 337, X20 = 0 for the first 14 peaks). That rules out residual Mn₃O₄—its scale independently collapses to zero—and also shows the Fe₂WO₆ topology is the wrong lattice despite partial peak overlap. I’ll use this indexed metric to find the closest W–O framework rather than forcing the earlier analogue.

The residual family now has a strong orthorhombic indexing solution. I’m testing the chemically supported Mn₂WO₆ P2₁2₁2₁ model against the MnWO₄ + WO₃ checkpoint; acceptance will require a stable positive scale, near-indexed cell, physical local geometry, and broad residual-family coverage.

The new phase is refining to a positive ~5–6 wt% scale and the indexed cell is staying close (volume change under 0.5%), so it passes the first stability check. I’m waiting for the profile-width stage, which is the decisive test for whether it removes the full residual family rather than borrowing isolated peaks.

The topology is real enough to hold a positive scale, but its parent coordinates leave the strongest indexed reflections badly underpredicted and push its crystallites to the imposed 40 nm floor. I’m therefore treating this as an indexed lattice/topology lead—not yet an accepted phase—and checking the frozen index for an exact or isostructural framework with the same metric before attempting coordinate repair.

The indexed family contains strong 100, 003, and 030 reflections, which P2₁2₁2₁ forbids. That is decisive: the Y₂WO₆ analogue has the right gross chemistry but the wrong symmetry/topology. I’m now screening same-stoichiometry oxide frameworks that permit those reflections, adapting only their metric to the independently indexed cell before any Rietveld refinement.

The broader framework screen is still running. The key discriminator is now symmetry, not a marginal Rwp change: any candidate that cannot generate the observed odd axial reflections is excluded before refinement, even if overlapping peaks let it lower the global residual.

A chemically coherent framework has emerged: Mn₂BiO₅ (Pbam) → Mn₂WO₅ by Bi→W. Unlike the rejected P2₁2₁2₁ model, this topology permits the indexed odd axial reflections, has physical Mn–O/W–O geometry after metric adaptation, and gives the best physically admissible residual-profile correlation. Its stoichiometry also closes the 1:1 Mn:W feed exactly with residual WO₃. I’m moving it to a full three-phase Rietveld checkpoint now.

