The first Pbam analogue has an unavoidable 2.22 Å symmetry-related Mn–Mn contact because its borrowed Mn site is incompatible with the short 4.446 Å axis. I will not submit that topology. The lattice and Pbam extinction class remain secure, so I’m searching Pbam references with the same actual metric for a physically transferable cation-site arrangement before considering lower-symmetry coordinate freedom.

The residual lattice is now secure; the open question is its physically valid Mn-rich topology. I’m screening lower-symmetry Mn₂WO₅ analogues and checking the frozen generator’s allowed phase construction so I can replace the unphysical Pbam placeholder rather than merely improve its numerical fit.

The search is deliberately topology-aware: each candidate must explain the indexed reflections and retain plausible W–O, Mn–O, cation–cation, and O–O distances after transfer into the measured cell. This should distinguish a genuine lower-symmetry relative from a profile-only coincidence.

The candidate library is larger than expected because ordered ABCO₅ frameworks create several chemically distinct Mn/Mn/W assignments. I’m letting the full screen finish rather than pruning by apparent space-group labels; the latter are exactly where pseudosymmetry can hide the needed weak reflections.

