The P‑1 subgroup generates many exactly overlapping powder reflections, so direct least-squares cell refinement is numerically singular. I’ve fixed the independently indexed metric and am refining only the well-conditioned scales, accepted-phase cells, instrument, and phase widths; this keeps the symmetry test scientifically meaningful without interpreting a numerical failure as rejection.

The subgroup comparison is narrowing: both physically plausible low-symmetry families outperform the high-symmetry Pbam reference, and the La₂RuO₅-derived arrangement currently leads slightly. I’m completing the matched Pbam-subgroup control before touching coordinates, so any later gain can be attributed to topology rather than different profile treatment.

The La₂RuO₅-derived P‑1 model has a much lower structure-independent ceiling (Rwp 8.23%) than Pbam (9.46%), confirming that the odd reflections belong to this lattice. I’m now testing only ±0.10 Å coordinate neighborhoods; the key acceptance criterion is whether the intensity gain comes with repaired W–O geometry and stable positive abundance.

The bounded coordinate branch improved Rwp but every candidate coordinate ran into its ±0.10 Å limit and W–O shortened to ~1.50 Å; I’m rejecting that branch as nonphysical/nonconverged. The next check broadens the chemistry/topology search across all low-symmetry oxide frameworks, because that result says the correct answer is not a small perturbation of the La₂RuO₅ coordinates.

The broad pass starts from 22,288 chemically possible substitutions, then filters by no-translation symmetry, cell shape, density, and local coordination before any expensive diffraction calculation. This is a one-time exhaustive check aimed at finding a physically cleaner low-symmetry parent—not an invitation to keep adding phases.

The physical-library screen leaves one clear intensity match: Mn₂WO₅ on the La₂RuO₅/CaBeAsO₅ family; alternative low-symmetry stoichiometries score substantially worse. I’m using grouped Le Bail intensities to relocate only the six heavy-atom sites first, which is much better constrained by Cu X-rays than attempting dozens of oxygen coordinates at once.

