The physically constrained heavy-atom search did not beat the low-temperature La₂RuO₅-derived Mn₂WO₅ model, so I am stopping topology search as directed. I’m now completing the ordinary specimen model—zero/height, phase sizes and strain, instrument shape, polarization, and only data-supported roughness/texture—before the single prescribed coordinate polish.

The complete-profile branch is computationally heavier because zero and specimen height, phase broadening, and instrument shape must settle together. The refinement remains within the laboratory bounds; after this pass I will retain optional roughness, strain anisotropy, or texture only when each gives a reproducible profile gain.

The fully coupled nuisance refinement exposed a strong zero/height correlation and degraded the fit, so I rejected that branch. I’m switching to controlled one-term checkpoint tests; each specimen term must improve the incumbent on its own without hitting bounds before it is retained.

